fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1127", description: some(value: "`where`/`join` row-condition operands accept only Int/Float/Bool/String LITERALS — a let-bound value or an operation result is a LOAD ERROR. This is the gate WI-735 named and MIS-ATTRIBUTED to `fix`; `fix` never had one (WI-735 rejected, its three wi727_fix_value_may_be_* tests pin that), so the gap this ticket owns went ownerless for a moment and is re-filed here from its real site. MECHANISM: `compile_operand` (rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs) — the operand compiler inside the compile-time macro `guarded_of` (WI-722, proposal 052) — accepts exactly three shapes: a binder field access `c.x` (→ a query HOLE named by the field symbol, filled at runtime by where_run from the real schema), a bare binder `c` (→ the WHOLE_ROW_HOLE), and `Expr::Const` of Int/Float/Bool/String (→ the literal Value). EVERY other operand is `macro_rejects`. SHARED PREDICATE — CENSUS: compile_operand is reached from compile_condition, which BOTH `guarded_of` (→ where_run) and `conjoin_of` (→ join_run) call, so the restriction binds `where` AND `join` conditions alike; a fix must be measured on both. MEASURED (probe, current tree): 'r.where(lambda c -> eq(c.age, 30))' loads clean (the CONTROL); 'let v = 30' then 'r.where(lambda c -> eq(c.age, v))' AND 'r.where(lambda c -> eq(c.age, thirty()))' both fail at LOAD with '13:52: compile-time macro anthill.prelude.Relation.guarded_of cannot expand this expression: expected a column (c.x) or a literal in the where condition, got an operand that is neither a row column nor a literal'. WHY IT IS NOT A ONE-LINER: the lambda is compiled AS SYNTAX at load time, so `v`'s VALUE does not exist yet — the macro cannot fold it. FIX DIRECTION (the LINQ model proposal 052 already cites): an operand that is neither a column nor a literal becomes a second kind of HOLE — a PARAMETER of the recipe — captured at the call and filled at runtime by where_run/join_run from the evaluated operand, exactly as a column hole is filled from the schema. The RUNTIME half already exists and is proven: `fix` embeds an arbitrary evaluated Value in the same guarded(query, eq(col, v)) and restricts correctly for an op result, a runtime parameter and a nominal entity. What is missing is only the compile-time CAPTURE channel. CONSEQUENCE WHILE OPEN: relation.anthill and wi727_fix_test.rs document 'fix(x: v) ≡ where(eq(x, v)) + project', which is FALSE for exactly the non-literal v that fix accepts and where refuses — the equivalence must stay qualified until this lands. ACCEPTANCE: a let-bound value and an operation result each work as a where-condition operand AND as a join-condition operand, selecting the same rows the literal spelling does (drive the values, do not assert 'it loads'); a genuinely row-DEPENDENT operand stays a loud rejection; the qualification in relation.anthill / wi727_fix_test.rs is removed in the same change; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: ["WI-714", "WI-727"]), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-18T06:48:35Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1127", author: "claude", content: "ACCEPTANCE CORRECTION — the description names TWO sites to un-qualify; there are FOUR, and shipping against a 2-site list would repeat the exact defect that produced this ticket (WI-735's rejection claimed a completed doc sweep after naming 2 of 3 producers, and the one missed was the most-read). Census, by grep for 'WI-1127' on the current tree: (1) stdlib/anthill/prelude/relation.anthill — the user-facing `operation fix` declaration, the '≈, NOT ≡, until WI-1127' paragraph; (2) rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs — relation_fix's 'ASYMMETRY WITH `where`, until WI-1127' paragraph; (3) rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi727_fix_test.rs — the module header's '≈, NOT ≡' note plus the let-bound test's doc; (4) rustland/anthill-core/tests/include/wi714_where_test.rs — wi1127_where_operand_must_be_a_literal_today, which PINS the current refusal and is DESIGNED TO FAIL when this lands; delete it rather than relax it, and its panic message says so at the site. Also restore '≈' to '≡' at (1) and (3) once the operand sets actually match. Acceptance trimmed to cargo-test only (was cargo-test + scaland-sbt-test by CLI default): relations are rustland-only and the sibling tickets WI-714 / WI-727 both carry cargo-test alone, so the scaland gate was unmeetable rather than merely unnecessary.", at: "2026-08-17T23:49:35Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1127", author: "user", content: "DELIVERED. The gate is lifted at its real site: `compile_operand` (eval/builtins.rs) gained a THIRD arm — an operand that is neither a column nor a literal, and reads no row binder, becomes a PARAMETER hole (`__anthill_row_param_N__`, a dunder no column or user field can spell) and its EXPRESSION is handed to the runner as a captured argument under that same interned label. `splice_query_runner` emits them as named args filling a new trailing variadic capture: `where_run[P](r, cond, ...params: P)` and `join_run[L, R, P](r1, r2, cond, ...params: P)`. Proposal 056's capture rather than a fixed slot, deliberately: the arity is the condition's, and the ZERO-parameter case (the overwhelming majority) is then the already-proven empty record `fix()` uses — an empty synthesized tuple literal was the one shape I could not show would type. `fill_column_holes` is renamed `fill_recipe_holes` and matches a parameter hole by exact interned Symbol, the same seam a column hole uses; the compile and runtime ends are minted from ONE symbol, so there is no index arithmetic to desync. A ROW-DEPENDENT operand stays a loud rejection (`mentions_binder`).\n\nMEASURED BACK-OUT (parameter arm restored to the literal-only `macro_rejects`): 2 passed, 10 failed. The first back-out failed ALL TEN, including the literal control — because the control shared a namespace with the captured arms, so one load error killed the file and the measurement could not tell 'the change is out' from 'the fixture is broken'. The literal controls now live in their OWN knowledge base; that is stated at the test-file header with the numbers.\n\nTESTS — new wi1127_condition_param_test.rs, 12 driving the values (never 'it loads'): let-bound / operation-result / runtime-parameter on where AND on join (the shared-caller census the description demanded); two same-typed parameters on different columns under an `and` spine, where (30,2)->carol and (30,1)->alice, so a mis-paired or shared slot gives a WRONG ANSWER rather than a type error; row-dependent rejection on BOTH macros with a `bump(30)` control in the same shape; a BigInt literal, which used to be the explicit 'unsupported literal kind' refusal and now rides the parameter channel (so the four folded kinds are an OPTIMIZATION, not the admissible set); and binder matching is symbol-exact AND descends — `apply1(lambda c -> c, 30)` shadows the row binder's spelling and is CAPTURED, `apply1(lambda z -> c.age, 1)` reads the row from a nested lambda body and is REJECTED.\n\nDOC CENSUS — the feedback named FOUR sites; there were SEVEN. The four: (1) relation.anthill's `fix` declaration, (2) relation_fix's header, (3) wi727_fix_test.rs's header + the let-bound test, (4) wi714_where_test.rs's pin test — DELETED as instructed. The three the census missed, all of which STATE THE RULE rather than the qualification: (5) the `where` declaration's own doc in relation.anthill, which described the operand set and would have kept saying 'a literal -> a term constant' alone; (6) `guarded_of` + `compile_condition` in builtins.rs; (7) proposal 052's tree->query TABLE, which is where the rule actually lives — a row added for the captured operand plus the normative 'an operand is admissible iff it does not read the row'.\n\nCORRECTION TO THE FEEDBACK'S 'restore ≈ to ≡': there was never a `≈` in git. The qualification was written by the SAME uncommitted working tree this delivery commits, so `≡` -> `≈` -> `≡` nets to nothing in history. I first wrote 'the ≈ it was written as while they differed' at three sites; /code-review caught it as fabricated archaeology — a reader grepping for the old spelling finds nothing, and it converts a real recorded defect into a fictional record of prior care. All three now state the substantive fact: the `≡` this repo has ALWAYS asserted only became TRUE with this ticket.\n\n/code-review (high) found no correctness defect and ran 4415 tests clean, with its own probes (two `where`s in one op minting the same label — records do not cross; nested `where` in an operand; whole-row hole beside a param; params under or/not; effect propagation through the capture record). Its five findings were all accuracy defects in MY new prose, and all five are fixed: (a) the claimed shadowing OVER-rejection does not happen — binder symbols are scope-distinct, so the doc asserted a safety property the code does not have; I re-measured it myself and turned it into the permanent test above; (b) the fabricated `≈` history, at three sites; (c) 'the three wi727_fix_value_may_be_* tests' — there are five; (d) 'the ONE synthesizer of an Expr::Apply carrying named args' is false and contradicted by the note it cites (`substitute_to_occurrence` carries them, and `normalize_variadic_capture` rebuilds one) — restated as the property that actually matters to the WI-805 guard: it is the only one that MINTS its labels; (e) `fill_recipe_holes`'s header claimed a `Value::Node` in a goal is always a loud error, while the new parameter branch returns before that guard — the claim is about the RECIPE's own structure, not about what a parameter carries (a captured value is arbitrary runtime data, spliced verbatim exactly as relation_fix splices its argument), and now says so.\n\ncargo-test green via scripts/test.sh: 35 suites, 5117 passed, 0 failed.\n\nNOT DONE, and it has no owner: docs/kernel-language.md carries NO section on the Relation algebra at all — no `where`, no row lambda, no `guarded_of` — so there was nothing there to update. That is a pre-existing gap (WI-1073's audit did not reach 052) and a full spec section for the algebra is a ticket of its own, not a rider on this one.", at: "2026-08-18T06:48:26Z")

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1127", author: "user", content: "CORRECTION TO THE DELIVERY NOTE ABOVE — 'NOT DONE, and it has no owner: docs/kernel-language.md carries NO section on the Relation algebra' IS WRONG, and there is no gap. USER, 2026-08-18: 'this is not kernel-language, this is standard API which should be described in stdlib comments on Relation sort.' kernel-language.md is the KERNEL LANGUAGE — namespace/sort/rule/operation, the type and resolution rules. `Relation` is a STANDARD-LIBRARY SORT, and its algebra (where / join / project / fix / negate / union) is an API, so its documentation belongs on the declarations themselves in stdlib/anthill/prelude/relation.anthill. That is exactly where this ticket put the new operand rule — the `where` declaration's own doc now carries 'AN OPERAND IS ADMISSIBLE IFF IT DOES NOT READ THE ROW', with the capture and the row-dependent rejection stated beside it — so the site was right and only my reading of it was wrong. Nothing to file, nothing outstanding. The general rule I had mis-applied: [[proposals-keep-their-text-spec-absorbs-it]] says an implemented PROPOSAL's LANGUAGE change must reach kernel-language.md; a proposal that lands a library sort has no language change to absorb, and looking for one manufactures a spec gap out of an ordinary API doc.", at: "2026-08-18T07:14:11Z")
